# Customer-Facing Copy Only

## Absolute rule

**Never put internal brief language on the website.**

Briefs, MD specs, Linear tickets, and Slack notes often include production notes for the team (e.g. "The credibility section", "Prose-led, not bulleted", "This is where a prospect decides…", "Add named case studies if approved"). Those are instructions **to us**. They are never UI copy, never a dek, never a subhead, never helper text.

## What ships

Only ship text that is clearly marked as customer-facing in the brief:

- H1 / H2 / section labels when given as copy
- Body paragraphs, FAQs, CTAs, pricing lines
- Comparison-table cell values when specified for the page

If a line sits **above** the labeled fields (H2, Body, CTA) and reads like a job description for the section, it is internal. Leave it out.

## When copy is missing

- Do **not** invent a dek that restates the brief's meta note
- Prefer no supporting line over a made-up one
- If a short line is truly needed for layout, draft real customer language and flag it as new copy, never paste the production note

## Self-check before commit

Ask: "Would a prospect think this was written for them?" If it sounds like a note to the designer or strategist, delete it.
